# Getting Started, part 1: what a system needs.  The numbers are those of the
# measurements, and a test compares them with the document of the measurements.
.h1 What you need [need]
Strata is a set of four programs that work together: the Document Manager, the
Editor, the Printer and the Viewer.  The Editor and the Document Manager come in
two versions: the {b:terminal version}, which runs in a text terminal, and the
{b:window version}, which opens a window of its own on an X11 display.  This
chapter lists the software that they need, the desktops and the terminals that
are known to work with the keys that each one keeps, and the alternative
keybindings, which work in every one of them.
.h2 Software prerequisites [software]
Strata runs on Linux.  It was written and tested on Debian and Ubuntu on a PC
with a 64-bit processor (x86-64), and it also runs on Windows 11 through WSL2
with its Editor window.  Another distribution of Linux that has the same
programs should work, but it has not been tested.
.table 17 10 33
Software | Required or optional | What needs it, and what is lost without it
A terminal that reads UTF-8, or an X server | Required | The terminal version
 needs the terminal, the window version the X server and a screen of at least
 1024 by 1000 pixels.  Without one of them Strata cannot be used.
A fixed-width font with line characters | Required | DejaVu Sans Mono,
 Liberation Mono, Cousine or FreeMono.  Without such a font the lines and bars
 of the screen are drawn as empty squares.
LaTeX | Optional | Printing to a PDF file or to a printer, Preview, and the
 drawing of equations, LaTeX markers and keycaps as images in the window
 version.  Without LaTeX Strata works, but nothing can be printed or previewed.
 The equations, LaTeX markers and keycaps are shown in their short form.
{c:dvipng} | Optional | Drawing equations, LaTeX markers and keycaps as images
 in the window version; LaTeX makes them and {c:dvipng} turns them into
 images.  Without it they stay in their short form.
An X display for the Viewer | Optional | Preview shows its pages in the Viewer,
 which needs an X display even when you work in a terminal.
ghostscript | Optional | Printing a picture that is a PostScript or EPS file.
 Without it the Printer reports the picture as one that cannot be converted.
A print queue of your system | Optional | Printing on paper.  Without a queue
 a document is printed to a PDF file.
{c:aspell} or {c:hunspell}, and the dictionary of your language | Optional | The
 spell check.  The dictionary is a package of its own, such as {c:aspell-en} or
 {c:hunspell-en-us} on Debian and Ubuntu.  Without them a box shows that no
 spelling checker was found.
Thesaurus data | Optional | Tools, Thesaurus and {k:Ctrl-T}.  The data is a pair
 of files in the MyThes format, one with the extension {c:.dat} and one with the
 extension {c:.idx}, for one language.  On Debian and Ubuntu the package
 {c:mythes-en-us} puts the American English pair {c:th_en_US_v2.dat} and
 {c:th_en_US_v2.idx} in the directory {c:/usr/share/mythes}, which is searched
 for the data.
 Without the data a box shows that the thesaurus is not installed.
{c:curl} and {c:unzip} | Optional | Install Now in the list of fonts, which
 fetches a font from the Internet and unpacks it.  Without them the font is
 not installed.
.end
Everything in the table that is marked optional can be left out: Strata works
without it, and only the feature that needs it is missing.  The programs work
without LaTeX; without it, nothing can be printed to a PDF file or to a printer
and Preview cannot be shown.  Editing and saving need nothing besides the
Editor.  When a program or the data that a feature needs is missing, a box
names what is missing and the package to install, and everything else goes on
working.  The install script run by an administrator installs the programs
after a question; the install script run by anybody else installs Strata
whether or not they are there, and lists the missing ones in a warning at the
end, as the next chapter tells.  To list which of the programs your system
has, run the install script with the option that only lists them; nothing is
installed:
.code
scripts/strata-install --list-prereqs
.end
.h2 The programs [package]
.table 22 52
Program | What it does
The Document Manager | The menu that you start first.  It opens a document in
 the Editor, keeps the settings of all the programs, the list of fonts and
 the word lists of the spell check, and compares two versions of a document.
The Editor | The program in which you write.  It controls every part of the
 work on a document: typing, formatting, the spell check, previewing and
 printing.
The Printer | Prints a document through LaTeX, to a PDF file or to a printer
 of your system.  It is a program of its own, and the Editor starts it.
The Viewer | Shows the pages of a Preview in a window of its own.
.end
.h2 Desktops and window managers [desktops]
A desktop or a window manager can keep some keys for itself, so that the Editor
never receives them.  Every command that has a key can also be started in the
way of {x:keys-conflict}.  This section names each desktop that has been used
with Strata, and says which keys it keeps.  The window version of the Editor
draws with scalable fonts, and uses DejaVu Sans Mono when the system has it;
without a scalable font it uses a fixed font of the X server, with no true bold
and italic faces.
.h3 WindowMaker [desk-windowmaker]
WindowMaker takes none of the keys of Strata.  It has been used with the window
version of the Editor and the Document Manager, and everything works.
.h3 Microsoft Windows 11 with WSL2 [desk-windows]
Strata works on Windows 11 under WSL2, with the window version of the Editor on
the display that WSLg provides.  Opening a large document, such as the
Reference Manual, and Print Preview work.  Writing files and the exchange
between programs are slow under WSL2, most of all while the Printer writes its
LaTeX file, so printing takes longer than on a Linux machine.  Keep your
documents in the Linux file system, in your home directory, and not under
{c:/mnt/c}.
.h3 The X11 window with no window manager [desk-x11]
The window version of the Editor, run with no window manager, receives every
key of Strata.
.h2 Terminals [terminal-needs]
The terminal version draws its screen in one of three ways.  This part says what
each of them looks like ({x:term-screens}), what a terminal needs
({x:term-needs}), how to see and to change what Strata chose
({x:term-choose}), what to do when the characters look wrong
({x:term-check}), and describes each terminal that has been used with Strata:
{x:term-xterm}, {x:term-tmux} and {x:term-xfce4}.  Another terminal is covered
by {x:term-others}.
.h3 The three screens [term-screens]
Which of the three screens you get depends on your terminal and your locale.
.table 18 54
Screen | The shapes
UTF-8 | Double lines and single lines, shaded and solid blocks, triangles and
 arrows: all the shapes of the character set.  The terminal must
 be in UTF-8 (your locale is a UTF-8 locale, such as {c:en_US.UTF-8}), and its
 font must have the shapes.
VT100 | Single lines, drawn by the terminal itself, with the double lines
 turned into single lines, a checkerboard for the shaded blocks and the
 plain signs {c:>}, {c:<}, {c:^} and {c:v} for the triangles and arrows.  Strata
 writes ASCII characters only.
 Nearly every terminal speaks VT100, and no UTF-8 and no special font are
 needed.
ASCII | Plain characters that look like the lines and blocks: {c:+}, {c:-},
 {c:|}, {c:=} and {c:#}.  It works in every terminal.
.end
In all three the text of your document is the same.  The screen is only the way
that the terminal draws it: a letter with an accent, or a Greek letter, that the
VT100 and the ASCII screens cannot draw is shown as the nearest ASCII letter,
and the document keeps the letter, also when you save it.  The window version
of the Editor draws with its own fonts and has no such choice.
.h3 What a terminal needs [term-needs]
Any terminal that speaks VT100, which is nearly every terminal, can show the
VT100 screen.  The UTF-8 screen needs more.
- To read UTF-8.  Most terminals do when your locale is a UTF-8 locale.
- A fixed-width font that has the line, block and arrow characters, such as
  DejaVu Sans Mono, Liberation Mono, Cousine or FreeMono.  Squares with numbers
  in them, or empty squares, where lines and bars belong mean that the font does
  not have them; the cure is a better font, or the Terminal Characters field of
  Setup set to VT100.
A terminal needs one more thing for every screen: it must send {k:Esc} when you
press {k:Alt} with a key.  Redo ({k:Alt-/}), the soft hyphen ({k:Alt-_}) and many
other commands use {k:Alt}.

The terminal must have at least 80 columns by 25 lines; the Editor can be used
down to 40 columns by 20 lines.  The 16 colours of the terminal are used, and
more when {c:TERM} says {c:256color} or {c:COLORTERM} says {c:truecolor}; it
also works on a terminal with no colours.
.h3 How Strata chooses, and how to change it [term-choose]
At its start the program chooses the screen once, in this order.
- When the environment variable {c:STRATA_ASCII} is 1 or 0, or the field
  Terminal Characters of Setup is UTF-8, VT100 or ASCII, that is the screen.
  Nothing is asked of the terminal.
- When Strata runs inside {c:tmux} or {c:screen}, a UTF-8 locale means the UTF-8
  screen.  Any other locale means the VT100 screen when the type of terminal
  ({c:TERM}) has the line characters, and the ASCII screen when it has not.
- Otherwise, when the locale is a UTF-8 locale, the terminal is asked where its
  cursor is after a box character.  One column, or no answer, means the UTF-8
  screen.
- Otherwise (the locale is not a UTF-8 locale, or the terminal moved the cursor
  by more than one column) the VT100 screen is drawn when the terminal can: its
  type has the line characters in the terminal database, or the terminal
  answers the question who it is as a VT100 or a later model.  If not, the
  ASCII screen is drawn.
When Strata chose the VT100 or the ASCII screen by itself, the message line of
the first screen says so once, for example {c:No UTF-8 terminal: VT100 lines are
drawn. See Setup, terminal_charset.}  The choice and its reason are also in the
log of the run.  To choose yourself, open Setup, find Terminal Characters, and
choose Automatic, UTF-8, VT100 or ASCII with {k:Left} and {k:Right}; or set
{c:STRATA_ASCII} to 1 (ASCII) or 0 (UTF-8) in the environment.
.h3 When the characters look wrong [term-check]
A screen that looks wrong has one of three causes: the terminal is not in UTF-8
and the UTF-8 screen was forced, the font lacks the shapes, or the terminal does
not speak VT100 although its type says so.  Run these two commands in the
terminal, outside Strata, to see what it does.  The first prints the top of a
box if the terminal speaks VT100:
.code
printf '\033(0lqqk\033(B\n'
.end
If it prints the letters {c:lqqk}, the terminal does not speak VT100: set
Terminal Characters to ASCII.  The second prints a horizontal line if the
terminal reads UTF-8 and its font has the shape:
.code
printf '\342\224\200\n'
.end
If it prints several odd letters, the terminal is not in UTF-8: set Terminal
Characters to VT100, or Automatic in a locale that is not a UTF-8 locale.  If it
prints a square, the font lacks the shape: use another font, or set Terminal
Characters to VT100.
.h3 xterm [term-xterm]
{c:xterm} has been used with Strata (version 407, run on a virtual display), and
each of these cases was run and its screen looked at.  The options of the third
case are {c:-lc -u8 -fa "DejaVu Sans Mono" -fs 11}, and {c:-fn fixed} chooses
the bitmap font of that name.
.table 24 48
Case | What the screen showed
1 No option, locale C | The VT100 lines, drawn right.
2 No option, UTF-8 locale | The double lines and the other shapes, drawn
 | right.
3 The options, locale C | The VT100 lines, as in case 1.
4 The options, UTF-8 locale | The double lines, as in case 2.
5 {c:-fn fixed}, locale C | The VT100 lines, drawn right.
6 {c:-fn fixed}, UTF-8 locale | Squares in place of the double lines, the
 | blocks and the arrows: this font does not have the shapes.  The field
 | Terminal Characters of Setup set to VT100 gave the right lines.
7 UTF-8 forced in the locale C | The box falls apart: each box character
 | shows as several odd letters, with or without the font DejaVu Sans Mono.
 | ({c:STRATA_ASCII=0}) Do not force UTF-8 there.
.end
So a plain {c:xterm} needs no option and no setting: in the locale C it shows
the VT100 lines, and in a UTF-8 locale the UTF-8 shapes.  The options of
case 3 changed nothing on the screen.  The one case that fails is a font that
lacks the shapes, and the cure is VT100 in Setup.  The keys are another
matter: when the commands that use {k:Alt} type a character, the resource
{c:XTerm*metaSendsEscape: true} makes {k:Alt} send {k:Esc} before the key, and
the keys that a terminal keeps for itself are listed in {x:appterm-reach}.
.h3 tmux [term-tmux]
{c:tmux} is a terminal multiplexer: it runs inside a terminal and shows another
one.  Start it in UTF-8 mode ({c:tmux -u}), or set the locale to a UTF-8 locale;
a {c:tmux} that is not in UTF-8 mode turns every character outside ASCII into an
underscore.  Run inside an {c:xterm}, {c:tmux} takes
one key of Strata, because {k:Ctrl-B} is its command key.  A multiplexer
translates the characters itself, so Strata chooses the screen from the locale
and the type of terminal and asks the terminal nothing ({x:term-choose}).
.table 18 20 22
Key the terminal takes | Strata command | Alternative Strata keybinding
{k:Ctrl-B} | Bold on and off. | {k:F12}, Edit, Layout, Attribute, then Bold.
.end
The command key of {c:tmux} can be changed in the file {c:~/.tmux.conf}, for
example with {c:set -g prefix C-]}, and {c:tmux} sends its own command key to
the program when it is pressed twice.  Both are documented by {c:tmux} and were
not tried with Strata.
.h3 xfce4-terminal [term-xfce4]
{c:xfce4-terminal} meets the three needs when its font is a fixed-width font
with the line characters, but it takes nine keys of Strata, which the table
lists in the order of the key tables of this manual.  Each of them can be
replaced by the alternative in the table.
.table 18 20 22
Key the terminal takes | Strata command | Alternative Strata keybinding
{k:F1} | Help. | {k:F12}, Help.
{k:Shift-Insert} | Append a subsection. | {k:Alt-F2}, then Sub-section.
{k:Shift-PageUp} | Previous section of the same level. | {k:Alt-F7}, then
 Previous section, of any level.
{k:Shift-PageDown} | Next section of the same level. | {k:Alt-F7}, then Next
 section, of any level.
{k:Shift-Home} | Start of the document. | {k:Alt-F7}, then Document start.
{k:Shift-End} | End of the document. | {k:Alt-F7}, then Document end.
{k:Shift-Up} | Previous section. | {k:Alt-F7}, then Previous section.
{k:Shift-Down} | Next section. | {k:Alt-F7}, then Next section.
{k:Ctrl--} | Undo. | {k:Ctrl-Z}, {k:Ctrl-/} or {k:Ctrl-F10}.
.end
{c:xfce4-terminal} also takes {k:F10}, {k:F11}, {k:Shift-F10}, {k:Ctrl-Insert}
and the Menu key, which carry no command of Strata.  Its preferences, on the
Shortcuts page, can release {k:F1}, {k:F10} and {k:F11}; that is described by
the program and was not tried.  No setting was found that releases the other
eight keys of the table, so use the alternatives.
.h3 Other terminals and remote logins [term-others]
A terminal that is not described here can keep keys that Strata uses; the ways
of {x:keys-conflict} work in every terminal.  Over {c:ssh}, a line that begins
with a tilde and a full stop ends the connection; type a space before the
tilde.
.h2 Alternative Keybinding [keys-conflict]
Every command that has a function key, and every command of the menu, can be
started without the key.  These ways work in every desktop, window manager and
terminal, because they use keys that none of them keeps.
.table 24 36
Way | What it does
{k:Ctrl-F} | Opens a list of every command that has a function key, with
 the key written beside it.  Choose a command with {k:Up}, {k:Down} and
 {k:Enter}.  The list also has the commands of {k:Alt-[}, {k:Alt-]} and the
 soft hyphen.
{k:F12} | Opens the menu bar: File, Global, Edit, Print, Tools, Help and
 Quit.  Every command that has a function key is in it, with its key
 beside it: Copy, which is {k:F3}, is {k:F12}, Edit, Block, Copy.
{k:Alt-O} | Switches overtype.
.end
When your desktop or terminal keeps a key, press {k:F12} and choose the
command from the menu, or press {k:Ctrl-F} and choose it from the list.  When a
key carries a command that is not in either, such as {k:Ctrl-B} (Bold), the
Attribute menu has it ({k:F12}, Edit, Layout, Attribute).  Press the key twice
when tmux keeps it: {k:Ctrl-B}.
.h2 Memory, time and disk space [resources]
The memory and time numbers in this section were measured on a PC with an Intel
Core i7-6700HQ processor and 15 GB of memory (the sizes of the disk and of the
prerequisites on another PC), with the programs of the release build and with
the files of the document in the memory cache of the system.  A slower computer
takes longer in proportion to its processor; the memory does not depend on the
speed.  A page is 500 words, the length of a typed page; the Printer fits
about 735 words on a page of its default layout, so that a document of 1000
pages here prints as 679 pages.  The one page document is a letter of 380
words.
.h3 Memory of the Editor [memory]
The table is for the terminal version.  Each number is the peak memory of the
Editor, in megabytes (MB), after it opened a document of the size in the first
two columns, after you moved to the end of the document, and after you saved
it.
.table 8 10 12 14 12
Pages | Words | Memory after opening (MB) | Memory after moving to the
 end of the document (MB) | Memory after a save (MB)
100 | 50,035 | 4.6 MB | 5.1 MB | 5.4 MB
500 | 250,007 | 10.9 MB | 13.4 MB | 15.0 MB
1000 | 500,034 | 19.5 MB | 24.9 MB | 28.4 MB
2000 | 1,000,008 | 37.3 MB | 48.9 MB | 55.9 MB
3625 | 1,812,586 | 65.9 MB | 87.5 MB | 100.4 MB
.end
The memory grows in step with the document: twice the pages need about twice the
memory.  A document of up to 50 pages needs less than 5 MB.  The window version
needs about 10 MB more than the terminal version at every size.  The largest
document that was measured, the whole collection of the works of Mark Twain, is
a file of 15 MB and has 5,258 pages of 500 words (it prints as 3,625 pages); it
needs 95 MB to open and 145 MB after a save in the terminal version.
.h3 Time [time]
The times are in seconds (s), for the terminal version: the time to open the
document until the first screen is drawn, the time to search from the start to
the end of the document for a word that is found only at the very end, and the
time to save the document after typing one character.  The window version
takes between half a second and two thirds of a second to open, whatever the
size, because it starts a window.
.table 8 16 18 16
Pages | Time to open the document | Time to search the whole document |
 Time to save the document
100 | 0.045 s | 0.028 s | 0.034 s
500 | 0.141 s | 0.116 s | 0.139 s
1000 | 0.193 s | 0.174 s | 0.199 s
2000 | 0.301 s | 0.259 s | 0.307 s
3625 | 0.460 s | 0.396 s | 0.486 s
.end
.h3 Printing [print-time]
A document of plain text is printed to a PDF in about half a second for a
letter, and in 10.3 seconds, with 61 MB of memory, for 3625 pages.  A document
with a table of contents, an index, lists of figures and tables, references to
sections and pages, headers and footnotes needs two or three runs of LaTeX; they
are made automatically, and the references are all in place when the print
ends.
.table 8 10 6 14 14
Pages | PDF pages | Runs | Time to print (s) | Memory in MB
10 | 12 | 2 | 1.4 s | 50.8 MB
100 | 74 | 2 | 2.2 s | 51.0 MB
500 | 352 | 2 | 5.6 s | 51.6 MB
1000 | 703 | 2 | 9.8 s | 52.1 MB
3600 | 2535 | 3 | 47.8 s | 58.8 MB
.end
.h3 Disk space [disk]
The programs, the documents and the templates that the install puts in the
prefix directory take 5.4 MB.  The manuals as PDF files take 3.7 MB more, so
that the whole release is about 9 MB.
.table 36 14
What | Disk space
The programs | 3.0 MB
The documents of Strata | 2.2 MB
The templates of letters and envelopes | 32 KB
The licence and the README | 64 KB
The manuals as PDF files | 3.7 MB
.end
A document that you save keeps up to three backups (a setting), so that a
document of 15 MB takes up to 60 MB.  A print of 1,000 pages writes a LaTeX
file of about 3.6 MB and a PDF of 2.5 MB.
The programs that Strata needs in addition, LaTeX, ghostscript, the spell check
and the thesaurus, are 126 packages on a system that has none of them: a
download of 191 MB, and 661 MB when they are installed.  The download is up to
about 700 MB less whatever your system has already; the install script shows
the exact size for your system before anything is installed.
.h3 What to have [recommended]
For documents up to 5,000 pages, 256 MB of free memory is enough in both
versions: the Editor needs at most 155 MB for such a document and a print needs
at most 87 MB.  For a book of 1,000 pages 64 MB is enough.  Keep 700 MB of free
disk space if the programs that Strata needs are to be installed.
